
Introduction
===============================================================================

      Millions of messages or articles are posted each year in Usenet
      ranging across a wide variety of topics. Additionally, millions of
      people communicate using Internet e-mail.  With PCBoard and a Usenet
      feed, both information sources can be tapped.

      The configuration of the UUCP part of PCBoard consists of the
      following:

        o Obtaining a Usenet and e-mail feed through a provider
        o Configuring the relevant fields in PCBSetup
        o Creating and configuring the conferences which will exchange
          articles and e-mail
        o Creating an entry in the SYSTEMS file for your provider
        o Configuring one or more events to exchange mail and news


Getting Started
===============================================================================

  Contacting a Provider
  ---------------------
    With the explosive growth of the Internet, services helping you get
    connected are popping up all over the world.  Call into Salt Air and
    download a file called PROVIDER.ZIP for a list of Internet Providers.
    Additionally, you can ask around on local bulletin boards to see if
    anyone knows of a provider in the local area.

  What about domain and site names?
  ---------------------------------------
    The terms "domain name" and "site name" refer to different identification
    schemes.  Domain name identifies a site on the Internet while site name
    refers to a site in the UUCP network.  The Internet and UUCP systems are
    not one in the same although most bulletin boards carry both Internet
    e-mail and UUCP news.

    Now that you have obtained an Internet provider, you need to get the
    following information from them:

      1.  Your UUCP site name
      2.  Provider's UUCP site name
      3.  Your domain name
      4.  Compression program used for compressed and batched mail (used later)

      NOTE:  It is possible to transfer mail and news without your own
      domain name.  In this situation either sub or bang domain processing
      must be used.  More on this topic is discussed in the "PCBSetup"
      section of this document.

  Setting UUCP Options in PCBSetup
  --------------------------------
    The bulk of configuration for UUCP in PCBSetup is contained in the
    screen titled "UUCP Configuration" from PCBSetup's Main Menu.  For the
    purpose of this section, we assume you have some familiarity with
    the fields in PCBSetup.  If you want detailed explanations of these
    fields, refer to the PCBSetup section of this document or press F1 to
    access PCBSetup's online help.

    Let's begin be looking at the first section of the screen.  This section
    stores organization and path information:

        Organization : Salt Air BBS / Murray, UT  801-261-1686

        Base UUCP Path            : ..\UUCP\
        Spool Directory           : ..\UUCP\SPOOL\
        Log File Directory        : ..\UUCP\LOGS\
        Decompress Batch File     : ..\UUCP\DECOMP.BAT
        Moderator List File       : ..\UUCP\USENET.MOD

    Organization:  Enter information about your system including but not
    limited to the name, location, and phone.  This information is posted in
    the header of all e-mail and news messages exported from your system.

    Base UUCP Path:  Since this is the beginning subdirectory for all UUCP
    activity, it's best to keep this in a central location.  If you run
    in a network environment, specify a directory on the server (e.g.,
    F:\PCB\UUCP\, C:\PCB\UUCP\).

    Spool Directory:  An additional tree is created beneath the subdirectory
    specified in this field.  In fact, one subdirectory is created for each
    provider.  Large volumes of information are stored in the subdirectories
    specified so make sure PLENTY of FREE SPACE exists.

    Log File Directory:  Extensive logs are kept regarding importing,
    exporting, and transfer of messages.  These files will grow gradually
    so you should plan on deleting or archiving them regularly to prevent
    them from filling up the drive.

    Decompress Batch File:  This field specifies the batch file to run to
    uncompress batched messages from your provider.  The program you should
    run in this batch file depends largely on the information gathered from
    the provider in the previous question.  The two most popular compression
    programs are Compress (COMP430D) and GZip.  Your provider will either
    have or know where to get the proper decompression program to put in
    this batch file.

    Moderator List File:  Generally leave this field unmodified.  This
    file only has to be modified in very special circumstances since
    UUCP attempts to handle all moderation information automatically.

        UUCP Name                 :
        UUCP Domain Name          :
        UUCP Email Host           :
        UUCP News Host            :

    UUCP Name:  Enter YOUR UUCP site name obtained from the provider.

    UUCP Domain Name:  Enter YOUR domain name obtained from the provider.
    If no domain name is being used, you must enter the domain name of the
    provider.

    UUCP Email Host:  Enter the PROVIDER's UUCP site name in this field.

    UUCP News Host:  Enter the PROVIDER's UUCP site name in this field.

        Internet Email Conference :     0
        Usenet Junk Conference    :     0

    You must inform PCBSetup of two special conferences on your system.
    The first conference is where all Internet e-mail is imported into.  The
    second is the conference number where all unclassified mail is put.

    At this point we're ready to exit PCBSetup and save the configuration.
    It's time to edit/create the SYSTEMS file which is used to call the
    provider.

  Strategy for Developing the SYSTEMS File
  ----------------------------------------

    The SYSTEMS file is important to you because it controls all of the
    information necessary to call a provider.  Not only does it store
    information like the name of a provider but also phone number and
    instructions of how to logon.

    Editing the SYSTEMS File
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    The SYSTEMS file is simply an ASCII file and is stored in the Base UUCP
    Path.  To edit the file, a text editor must be used.  When this file is
    edited for the first time, it will resemble this:

# The # comments out a line in the file
# try modifying one of these lines to match your site
xmission f1 f2 f3 f4 g "" ATZ OK \dATDT5390900 CONNECT \c gin--gin Name word pwd

    This example SYSTEM file gives you some sense for what is required to
    create or modify an entry.  To fully understand this file you must
    realize that any line NOT beginning with a # represents an entry for a
    system.  Each entry consists of a set of fields which UUXFER will access
    and use to login to the system.  Use the descriptions that follow to help
    you make an entry for your provider.

       1. xmission - Quite simply, this is the site name UUXFER will look up
          based on what you pass via the -s command line parameter.  In this
          case, a site named xmission is being defined.  This name refers to
          your Internet provider.

       2. f1 - While this field is not used by UUXFER, other programs
          similar to UUXFER use it to determine valid times of the day to
          call.

       3. f2 - This field is not used by UUXFER but similar programs use it
          to determine the serial port to use for communications.

       4. f3 -  Some programs use this field to determine what type of
          serial interface to use, but UUXFER obtains this information from
          PCBoard.

       5. f4 - While UUXFER does not use this field, other similar programs
          use it to control the opening port rate.  Instead, this
          information is obtained from PCBoard.

       6. g - This field represents the protocol to use.  At this time,
          UUXFER uses the widely used g protocol.  Once you have the entry
          working and transferring mail/news you may want to adjust a
          parameter of the protocol to increase performance. The defaults
          are to use a packet size of 64 bytes and a window size of 3
          packets.  To override the defaults, put a two digit number to the
          right of the g (i.e., g34 or g32). The first digit controls the
          size of the transfer window and must be 1 through 7 (the default
          is 3 if unspecified).  The second digit controls the size of the
          individual packets and must be 1 through 8 (for packet sizes of
          32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, or 4096, respectively; the
          default is 2 if unspecified).

       7. Everything to the right of the protocol specification is
          considered the chat script.  What the chat script amounts to is a
          string of text to expect and a string of text to send when the
          expected text is found.  For example, I will have the script wait
          for login.  When that text is received, send my login name.  This
          is expressed in the following manner on the system line:

            login yourbbs   (where yourbbs is the login name for the system)

    Before proceeding to the next section, make sure you have an entry in
    the SYSTEMS file comprising of the first 6 fields.  Example:

      provider f1 f2 f3 f4 g

    With this much information entered for your site, go ahead and save the
    file and exit.  We're ready to write the chat script.

    Writing the Chat Script
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Writing the chat script requires three major steps.  First, you must
    capture an actual logon attempt to a text file.  Second, an
    understanding of special characters utilized in a chat script is
    required.  Finally, we'll use the text file created in step 1 and the
    knowledge acquired in step 2 to write the chat script.

      Capturing a Login to a Text File
      ................................
      To capture a manual login session with your Internet provider, you'll
      need to load your communications program (Procomm Plus, Telix, etc.)
      Once it is loaded, type "ATE1 <ENTER>" which enables echo mode
      on modem commands.

      Next, turn on the capture log.  For most communications programs,
      this is done by pressing ALT-L.  The program you are using may differ so
      consult your manual if questions arise.

      Before calling your system, make sure you know the user ID and
      password required to login.  Without it, the next 5 steps cannot
      be completed.

          1.  Issue the command to dial your internet provider.  Typically,
              this is ATDT followed by the phone number of the provider.
              (e.g., ATDT 555-1212)  Once the phone number is entered,
              press ENTER to begin dialing.  If it's busy, continue trying
              at regular intervals.

          2.  Assuming your connected at this point, the first prompt you
              see is going to be a login prompt.  It may look something
              like this:

                 (slc-ts1 port 0) provider.com login:

               NOTE:  Some sites may have a menu you have to navigate before
               logging into the system.  Go ahead and select the menu option
               which allows you to login.

          3.  Type in the login name for your UUCP account.  Once the name
              is properly entered, you're typically asked for a password.
              Enter the required password in this field.

          4.  At this point, you should see something like "SHERE" appear on
              your screen.  This is the beginning handshake sequence for
              sending mail and news.  Congratulations!

          5.  Close the log file because are task is completed.

      Special Commands/Sequences in a Chat Script
      ...........................................
      Recall that the chat script is nothing more than a sequence of strings
      to expect and strings to send once a match is found.  These sequences
      are separated with spaces.  Example:

           <expect> <send> <expect> <send> <expect> <send>

      Occasionally, a special character will be used in one of these strings
      to delay for a second or two, send a carriage return or even a space
      (since a space character separates an expect/send sequence).

      This section is dedicated to telling you about the most common special
      characters and their uses.  The last part of the section will discuss
      the lesser used characters in case your situation requires their usage.

      The most common special characters are:

         "" - Placeholder for nothing.  Represents a place holder for nothing.
              Typically this is used in the expect portion of the sequence
              meaning not to expect anything.  This will always match so
              whatever is to be sent out will be.  A good example of this is
              the sequence which initializes the modem before dialing out:

                 "" ATZ

              If you do not want to send anything out, you'll also have to
              suppress the carriage return automatically entered to every
              outgoing response.

         \c - Suppress Carriage Return.  Every response sent out via the
              chat script has a carriage return automatically appended to
              the end.  For example, if you tell the chat script to send
              "Triangle", it will send "Triangle <cr>" where the <cr> is a
              carriage return or ENTER key.  Example:

                CONNECT /c

         \d - Delay two seconds.  Sometimes it is useful to have a chat
              script delay.  A delay is most often need when sending a
              response not when expecting something.  This point can be
              illustrated best when initializing the modem and waiting
              for an OK to come back.  Upon receiving the OK, you'll send
              the dial string.  The problem is that some modems have a
              problem immediately receiving a command after responding to
              one.  Therefore, adding a delay before dialing is wise.
              Example:

                OK /d/d"ATDT555-1212"

         \r - Send a carriage return.  When you need to send a carriage
              return, use this special code.  Remember, there is already a
              carriage return sent with EVERY sequence unless the /c code is
              sent.

      The following lists sums up the rest of the special characters and
      a brief description of each:

        \8    - Shift to 8N1 mode
        \b    - Backspace character
        \e    - Shift to 7E1 mode
        \K    - Send BREAK (NUL)
        \n    - Send the character for a new line
        \N    - Send NUL
        \p    - Delay a fractional second
        \q    - Quietly display send field
                ("\qpassword" to not display password)
        \s    - Space character
        \t    - Tab character
        \\    - Backslash character
        \###  - Any three octal digits to define any character
        \?    - Quotes any other character (denoted by ?)
        BREAK - Send BREAK (NUL)
        EOT   - Send EOT character

      Analyzing the Log File
      ......................
      It is now time to look at the log file we gathered in the first
      step and combine that with our knowledge of the special chat script
      codes to complete our chat script.  To do this task, we will look
      at a few lines of the log file at a time to see if we can decipher
      what we need to do.

      Let's begin by looking at what it will take to dial the phone number:

         ATZ
         OK
         ATDT5551212

      According to our sample log file, the first thing we do is initialize
      the modem followed by dialing our site.  As far as the chat script
      is concerned, this is expressed as:

        "" ATZ OK /dATDT5551212
        ------ ----------------
          1           2

      Notice the use of two special characters--one in each sequence.
      Remember the delay is just to keep the modem the happiest.  Otherwise,
      your dial attempt may fail.

      Proceeding on, let's see what we have to analyze next:

         CONNECT 9600/ARQ/V32/LAPM/V42BIS

         (slc-ts1 port 0) indirect.com login:yourname

      Here we have two things going.  First of all, we have the response from
      our modem telling us we connected.  Immediately after that we have
      the login prompt where we type our name.

      In our chat script we want to watch for both items.  When we find the
      CONNECT string we're not going to send anything back.  With the login
      prompt though, we'll need to send our login name.  The following shows
      what our chat script looks like now:

        "" ATZ OK /dATDT5551212 CONNECT /c gin:--gin: yourname
        ------ ---------------- ---------- -------------------
          1           2             3              4

      On initial inspection you're probably wondering what is going on with
      sequence #4.  According to our capture file we will never see the text
      "gin:--gin:".  You are correct.  What we have done though is bought
      a little bit of insurance.  This expect sequence will search for
      'gin:' for the timeout period (default of 60 seconds).  If it is not
      found, a carriage return will be sent and the chat script will attempt
      to find 'gin:' again.  On the off chance that it is not found, the
      script will abort after the second try.

      Let's look at the final part of our capture file--the password prompt:

        Password:

      This one is the easiest segments we had to write for.  Here we have
      to basically do what we did for the login prompt.

        word:--word: pwrd

      We look for the text "word:" up to two times.  If we find it, we'll
      send a password of 'pwrd'.  With this final piece in place, our
      chat script looks like this:

       "" ATZ OK /dATDT5551212 CONNECT /c gin:--gin: yourname word:--word: pwrd
       ------ ---------------- ---------- ------------------- -----------------
         1           2             3              4                  5

      With the chat script done, we need to test it out to make sure it works
      okay.  This is the theme of the next section.

    Testing The SYSTEMS File
    ------------------------
    With the entry in the SYSTEMS file complete, it becomes important to
    test it.  By going to a directory with a PCBOARD.DAT and running UUXFER,
    we can find out if our entry works correctly or not.  By default, UUXFER
    calls all systems.  To test a single site, use the -s command line
    parameter.  Assuming the entry we made in the previous section is
    for a site named "provider", type:

        uuxfer -sprovider

    If a screen appears but then quickly disappears it could be due to any
    of the following problems:

       1) There is no PCBOARD.DAT in the current directory
       2) You did not specify the -s parameter with a site name to call.
       3) The site name specified is incorrect.  In other words, an entry
          for it does not exist in the SYSTEMS file.

    Ultimately, the UUXFER.LOG file will tell you why it did not complete.
    Please refer to that file to assure yourself of the reason for not
    running as expected.

    When everything is done properly in regards to loading UUXFER, you'll
    see a screen similar to the following:

 ÚÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ UUXFER Version 1.10 -- UUCP/UUCICO Utility for PCBoard ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ¿
 ³ Processing Chat Script for PROVIDER                                        ³
 ³ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ³
 ³                                     ³³                                     ³
 ³                1                    ³³                2                    ³
 ³                                     ³³                                     ³
 ³ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ³
 ³                                     ³³                                     ³
 ³ ""             3                    ³³ ATZ                  4              ³
 ³ OK                                  ³³ \d\d\dATDT5551212                   ³
 ³ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ³
 ³ ATZ                                                                        ³
 ³ OK                                  5                                      ³
 ³                                                                            ³

    We have numbered each segment of the screen to make it easier to explain
    what you are seeing.

      1.  Displays g protocol information about the packets coming into the
          system.  This information may include items such as packet number,
          packet size, etc.

      2.  Similar to window 1, this window shows the information for packets
          sent from the system.

      3.  This window is very much a multi-purpose window meaning the
          information displayed varies depending on the progress of the
          call.  For our concern--testing the chat script--this region of
          the screen shows you what the chat script is expecting to find.
          In the example presented, the first thing searched for is ""
          (nothing), followed by "OK".

      4.  Like window 3, this window displays varying information.  While
          processing the chat script, it shows what text has been sent out
          to the modem.

    Because UUXFER gets its information from the PCBOARD.DAT file, make sure
    it is run in a configuration where it can dial out.  A node that is not
    configured for a modem is not a good place to test UUXFER.

    Closely watch the chat script progress.  If a problem is discovered while
    the chat script is processed, make the necessary changes to the script
    and try again.  As soon as the chat script is complete are you are logged
    into the provider, you should see the top of the screen change to read:

 ÚÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ UUXFER Version 1.10 -- UUCP/UUCICO Utility for PCBoard ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ¿
 ³ Performing UUCP G Protocol File Transfers for PROVIDER (Master Mode)       ³
 ³ÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄ³

   This message is our goal.  It lets us know that we successfully logged into
   the provider, negotiated the connected and began sending information using
   the G protocol.  Congratulations on setting up the SYSTEMS file and getting
   it working properly!

  Configuring / Creating the Conferences
  --------------------------------------
    The conferences on the system handle all the message traffic.  Topics
    of discussion are broken down into conferences.  In a UUCP configuration,
    a conference must be created for Internet e-mail, junk mail, and
    one for each newsgroup carried.

    These requirements amount to a lot of new conferences on your system.
    What we will guide you to do is setup these conferences.  What follows
    assumes knowledge of how to create a conference in PCBoard.  If this
    topic is unfamiliar, consult the index in the printed manual for
    additional information.

    When adding the conferences to the system, many SysOps like to make the
    starting number an even one.  A good example of this is making the first
    conference number 100, 200, or 1000.  Following this type of number
    scheme provides a good way to group conferences.

    For the purpose of this example, let's make the starting conference
    number 100.  We'll make conference 100 the "Junk Mail" conference and
    101 will send/receive the e-mail.

    Create conferences 100 and 101 and configure them to be clones of the
    Main Board (#0).  Once that is done, all that remains is to edit the
    location of the message bases and configure a few options.  The following
    breaks down all that must be done to configure the two conferences after
    being cloned.

    "Junk Mail" (Conference 100)

      1.  Change the name/location of the message base to point to a JUNK
          file in the base UUCP path.  For example, if PCBSetup says the
          base UUCP path is F:\PCB\UUCP\, enter the message location of
          F:\PCB\UUCP\JUNK.

      2.  Press PgDn to edit the conference options.

      3.  Change the "Type of netmail" option to 2 so PCBoard will know it is
          the UUCP junk conference.

      4.  Update the junk conference number in PCBSetup | UUCP Configuration
          to reflect the proper conference number.

    "Internet E-Mail" (Conference 101)

      1.  Change the name/location of the message base to point to a file
          named I-EMAIL in the base UUCP path.  For example, if PCBSetup
          says the base UUCP path is F:\PCB\UUCP\, enter the message
          location of F:\PCB\UUCP\I-EMAIL.

      2.  Press PgDn to edit the conference options.

      3.  Using the list below, set the fields listed in the left column to
          the response in the right column.

             Make All Messages Private         Y
             Force Echo on All Messages        Y
             Type of Netmail Conference        1
             Allow Internet (long) TO: Names   Y

    The procedure for adding the conferences for UUCP news and mail areas
    are:

      1.  Get an ACTIVE file from your coordinator.
      2.  Determine what areas you want to carry
      3.  Prepare PCBoard to handle the increased conference load
      4.  Use UUUTIL.EXE to add the additional conferences.

    That is the shortened list of what you have to do.  Over the next few
    paragraphs, detailed descriptions of each task is given.

      1. Talk to your provider.  They will have a copy of the ACTIVE file
         which lists newsgroups they carry.

      2. The ACTIVE file is nothing more than an ASCII file with a newsgroup
         name on each line.

         The next task is to trim down the list to include only those
         newsgroups which interest you or your callers.  Don't be afraid to
         be picky here as there are costs involved for carrying each.
         Trimming the list is done by loading the file into a text
         editor--any editor will do.   When you see a newsgroup you do not
         want, delete the line (CTRL-Y usually works).  When done, save the
         file and exit the editor.  That's all there is to it.

         Look at this sample segment of the ACTIVE file:

                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.cd-rom 0000011602 0000011565 y
                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.chips 0000019503 0000019443 y
                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.comm 0000007566 0000007546 y
                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.misc 0000020206 0000020148 y

         Deciding I do not want the ibm.pc.hardware.chips group, I'll use
         the text editor to make this section look like this:

                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.cd-rom 0000011602 0000011565 y
                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.comm 0000007566 0000007546 y
                comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.misc 0000020206 0000020148 y

         See how easy that was!

      3. Next we must confirm the PCBoard setup is properly configured for
         the hundreds of new conferences that will be added.  Make a
         determination for a starting conference number; most SysOps elect
         to start at an even number of 100.  Say for example the highest
         conference currently in use is 78.  The common thing to do is to
         start the conferences at 100.

         Once the starting conference number is determined, find out how
         many areas you'll be carrying and add it to the starting conference
         number. As an example let's assume you'll start the conferences at
         200 and will be adding 212 areas.   This makes your highest
         conference 412 so check PCBSetup | Configuration Options | Messages
         to see if the "Highest Conference Desired" is set to the an
         appropriate value.

         If you have to change the number, load System Manager | User Info
         File Maintenance | Change Conference Allocation to ensure the
         USERS.INF file is up-to-date is upgraded too.

      4. A utility is included with the PCBoard package called UUUTIL.  With
         this utility the tedious task of setting up conferences is greatly
         simplified.  Consult the following checklist before continuing:

           1.  You have an ACTIVE file and have trimmed it down to list
               only those areas you desire to carry.

           2.  An adequate number of conferences is configured in PCBSetup |
               Configuration Options | Messages.

           3.  You know the starting conference number for the conferences.

           4.  You have a copy of UUUTIL.EXE and know its location.

         Now you're ready to import the new conferences to your system. The
         command line for UUUTIL will be:

            UUUTIL /START:[conf] /IMPORT:[ACTIVE]

         The conference number entered after the /START parameter must be
         your starting conference number for the conferences.

         The next parameter passed is /IMPORT.  This parameter tells UUUTIL
         where it can find the ACTIVE file.  This file has information about
         the newsgroups you want to carry.

         NOTE:  The default message base locations configured by UUUTIL are
         put underneath the base UUCP path.  To help balance the number of
         files in a directory, UUUTIL creates subdirectories for each message
         base.  For example, if adding alt.tv.reruns, PCBoard will specify
         the location as G:\PCB\UUCP\A\T\RERUN001.

         Notice how the message base is constructed.  "alt" is abbreviated to
         the subdirectory "A", followed by "tv" being abbreviated to the "T"
         subdirectory, and finally the first five characters of "RERUNS" are
         taken and a 001 is appended (the number changes to prevent duplicates)

         A little practice in this naming convention will go a long ways.
         Therefore, let's see what type of directory structure is obtained
         if we carry the following newsgroups:

            alt.fan.oldshows
            alt.bubba.bo.bob.brain
            comp.bbs.pcboard
            soc.culture.brazil

            \PCB\UUCP\---A\
                      |   +---B\
                      |   |    +---B\
                      |   |        +---B\  (holds alt.bubba.bo.bob.brain)
                      |   +---F\           (holds alt.fan.oldshows)
                      |
                      +--C\
                      |   +---B\           (holds comp.bbs.pcboard)
                      |
                      +--S\
                          +---C\           (holds soc.culture.brazil)


         NOTE: If you run UUUTIL and the screen clears but nothing seems to
               have happened, make sure you are running UUUTIL from a
               directory where a valid PCBOARD.DAT for your system exists.

         When UUUTIL is running, you will see text indicating the progress
         of the import which resembles the following:

            Checking conference 100 ...
            Inserting (soc.culture.afghanistan) as conference 100 ...

            Checking conference 101 ...
            Inserting (soc.culture.african.american) as conference 101 ...

            Checking conference 102 ...
            Inserting (soc.culture.arabic) as conference 102 ...

            Checking conference 103 ...
            Inserting (soc.culture.argentina) as conference 103 ...

         Once a conference is "inserted" by UUUTIL, the following has
         occurred:

           1) The conference name has been updated

           2) The message base location has been updated.  The format for
              the messages file location is the first 5 characters of the
              conference name's last segment.  In our soc.culture.brazil
              name, the last segment is BRAZIL so we would use BRAZI for
              the first five characters.  The next three characters are
              simply a number to prevent duplicates.

           3) The "Echo Mail in Conference" is set to Y while the "Type of
              Netmail Conference" field is set to 4.

  UUCP Events
  -----------
    Obviously, we'll need to take advantage of PCBoard's events to regularly
    call our provider and exchange mail.  The goal of this section is to
    show you how to do this task.  We'll begin by creating a separate batch
    file on disk that all events can run followed by making the entries in
    the event editor.

    Let's begin with creating the DOUUCP.BAT file.  The purpose of this
    batch file is to run UUOUT (message export), UUXFER (message transfer),
    and UUIN (message import).  To create this file, change to your UUCP
    Base directory.  Use a text editor to create DOUUCP.BAT as shown in
    this example:

       F:\PCB\UUOUT.EXE
       F:\PCB\UUXFER.EXE -s ANY -b 20
       F:\PCB\UUIN.EXE

    Notice how simple this batch file is.  We scan for mail to export,
    transfer the mail, and finally import any mail we picked up during
    the transfer.

    NOTE:  The beauty of DOUUCP.BAT is that you can easily make a manual run
    by running this batch file.  With the bulk of effort out of the way,
    let's proceed to configuring the events in PCBoard.

    Go to the event editor (edit EVENT.DAT) in PCBSetup | Event Setup.  The
    normal scream looks like this:

                       Batch     Begin  End                         Last
             Act  Mod  File      Time   Time   SMTWTFS    Date      Date
             ÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ
        1)    Y    E   DAILY     06:45  08:00  YYYYYYY            00-00-00

    There is nothing really special about the UUCP events because their
    purpose is to simply run DOUUCP.BAT.  Let's look at a sample set of
    events to add:

                       Batch     Begin  End                         Last
             Act  Mod  File      Time   Time   SMTWTFS    Date      Date
             ÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ  ÍÍÍÍÍÍÍÍ
        1)    Y    S   UUXFER    01:00  04:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00
        2)    Y    S   UUXFER    05:00  04:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00
        3)    Y    S   UUXFER    09:00  12:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00
        4)    Y    S   UUXFER    13:00  16:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00
        5)    Y    S   UUXFER    17:00  20:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00
        6)    Y    S   UUXFER    21:00  23:59  YYYYYYY            00-00-00

    What we have done here is run an event every 4 hours.  Notice all of the
    batch file names are the same because we are doing the same thing during
    every event.

    While on one of the entries in the editor, press F2 to edit the batch
    file.  All the event batch file needs to do is run DOUUCP.BAT in your
    UUCP base subdirectory.  Once you modify the event batch file once,
    all 6 events inherit the changes.  With this in mind, our event batch
    file will look like this:

       F:\PCB\UUCP\DOUUCP.BAT

    It's that simple.  Now all six events are setup and you're ready to
    transfer mail every 4 hours.

PCBSetup
===============================================================================

      When the UUCP Configuration options is selected from the Main Menu,
      the screen displays the UUCP options and paths for PCBoard.  This
      information is used to determine where outgoing e-mail can be found
      and where incoming e-mail will be placed.  It also controls addressing
      information such as domain names and their format.

      For the fields on this screen to have any relevance, you must have
      access to an Internet/Usenet provider.  Your answer to the fields like
      Email Host, Domain Name, and News Host all assume the information can
      be obtained from your provider if required.

      Let's start by looking at the UUCP configuration screen and explain
      each field.

    Organization
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~

      With every message sent out from your system a description of your
      site is included in the message header.  The text you enter in this
      field is the description PCBoard will add to each message.  Common
      information to include is your BBS name, and a way to contact the
      system such as a phone number or IP address.

    Base UUCP Path
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      UUCP uses auxiliary files to control many things: alias name
      translation, determining which message headers are kept, etc.  What is
      important to understand is that these auxiliary files are stored in
      the directory labeled as the Base UUCP Path.  The default location is
      \PCB\UUCP.  Information about these files is available in the UUCP
      Programs section. Specifically, you'll be looking for files like
      ALIAS.IN, ALIAS.OUT, and HDRKEYS.LST.

    Spool Directory
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      The subdirectory entered in this field represents the base spool
      subdirectory.  Underneath this subdirectory is another
      subdirectory--one for each site/provider.  For example, assuming the
      name of your email host is site1 and the base subdirectory is
      C:\PCB\UUCP\SPOOL\, the spool subdirectory for this site is
      C:\PCB\UUCP\SPOOL\SITE1.  UUOUT and UUXFER will create these
      subdirectories as needed.  The only time you will have to create the
      spool directory is if your feed is coming off of a satellite service
      such as PageSat or Planet Connect.  The following shows two examples
      of directory trees.

      The tree on the left is representative of a system where the email and
      newsgroups are picked up from one site. On the right is a tree
      representing a BBS where the newsgroup feed is put into the SATELLIT
      subdirectory, and the SITE1 subdirectory is used for all e-mail and
      outbound newsgroup articles.

      It is important to remember that a subdirectory must exist underneath
      the base UUCP directory for every site you interact with (either
      download email/news from or upload email/news to.)

      NOTE:  UUOUT, the exporting utility, will automatically create the
      spool files as required.  The only time you must concern yourself with
      creating them is when you have an inbound feed only such as a
      satellite service.

    Log File Directory
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      The log files for the various UUCP utilities such as UUIN, UUOUT, and
      UUXFER are stored in this subdirectory.  It is useful to look at log
      files when there is question to the activities performed by the
      programs.  For example, to see what messages were exported to the
      system last week.  Assuming the log file is not deleted daily, the
      information can be retrieved from the log files.

      These log files can grow quite large in size. Therefore, it would be
      wise for you to either backup or delete these log files on a regular
      basis.  Defining an event in PCBoard to do this daily, weekly, or
      monthly is a good idea.

    Decompress Batch File
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      In this field, enter the path and file name of a batch file to run to
      decompress batched news.  Generally, this will be a simple call to a
      single program.  Included with the PCBoard package is a sample
      DECOMP.BAT in the base UUCP subdirectory.  This batch file makes a
      call to a program called COMP430D.EXE which, due to the unknown
      origin, we cannot distribute with PCBoard.  COMP430D is used by
      several providers but the assumption cannot be made that it will work
      for all of them.  If in doubt or if you experience problems, contact
      your provider for information on the compression program used for
      batched news.  They should have the necessary software.

      Only one parameter, %1, is passed to this batch file. This parameter
      specifies the path and filename to decompress. Let's look at a sample
      DECOMP.BAT.

        @echo off
        cls
        c:\pcb\uucp\comp430d -db12 %1

    Moderator List File
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Some newsgroups are moderated.  This means that an article is not
      posted until the moderator reviews the message and determines if the
      content is appropriate.  If desired, you can maintain a list of
      moderators by pressing F2 while this field is highlighted.  Leave all
      entries in this file blank when UUCP should handle the moderating.

      NOTE:  UUCP will automatically send messages left in a moderated
      newsgroup to a central hub where it is then forwarded to the current
      moderator.  Therefore, it is best to let this be handled in the normal
      fashion unless circumstances dictate maintenance of your own list.

    UUCP Name
    ~~~~~~~~~
      In this field enter your UUCP name.  A UUCP site name must be
      registered by you or with the help of the service provider.

    UUCP Domain Name
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

      Every site has a domain name on the Internet.  When you setup an
      account with your provider this information should have been given to
      you.  Typically your domain name is a name consisting of lower case
      characters with dots as separators as shown in the following example:

        saltair.com
        m.cc.utah.edu
        npo.org

      Applying for a node name is your responsibility although many
      providers will either do the work for you or at the very least assist
      your efforts.  Contact them for additional information.

    UUCP Email Host
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      An e-mail host name refers to the name the provider uses to exchange
      e-mail.  Typically this name is 8 characters or less.

    UUCP News Host
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      In this field, enter the news host name of the provider where all
      outbound news articles are sent.

    News Distribution
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Enter the default news distribution for your site. (world for all
      sites, na for North America only, usa for USA, etc.)  If you are
      unsure, enter world.

    Time Zone Offset from GMT
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      This field is used to specify how many hours and minutes your site
      differs from Greenwich Mean Time.  Common values are:

        	AST -0400  ADT -0300
	EST -0500  EDT -0400
	CST -0600  CDT -0500
	MST -0700  MDT -0600
	PST -0800  PDT -0700

    Name Separator
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      This character is used to separate name elements when converting names
      from PCBoard format to Internet format.  For example, using a period
      (the default), the name FRED CLARK is converted to fred.clark.

    Internet Email Conference
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      All incoming e-mail is sent to the conference number you enter in this
      field.

    Usenet Junk Conference
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      This field contains the conference number into which Usenet junk is
      imported (primarily articles which do not have a valid newsgroup entry
      for the system).  Periodically looking at this conference helps insure
      all your conferences are setup for the news articles received.

    Bang Domain
    ~~~~~~~~~~~
      Normally, UUOUT will generate addresses of the form user@domain (i.e.,
      FRED CLARK at saltair.com becomes fred.clark@saltair.com).  Sometimes
      other processing is needed. If this field is set to Y, addresses are
      formed in the following fashion: host!user@domain.  With this switch
      enabled, the name you enter as the UUCP Domain Name is the domain name
      of your email host as illustrated in this example:

        UUCP Name                 : yourbbs
        UUCP Domain Name          : site1.com
        UUCP Email Host           : site1
        UUCP News Host            : site1

      A bang name for a user at this address will look like this example:
      yourbbs!user.name@site1.com.

      NOTE:  The bang domain or sub-domain name addressing is only used when
      you did not register a unique domain name through your provider.

    Sub Domain
    ~~~~~~~~~~
      Normally, UUOUT will generate addresses of the form user@domain (i.e.,
      FRED CLARK at saltair.com would become fred.clark@saltair.com).
      Sometimes other processing is needed. If this field is set to Y,
      addresses will be formed in the following fashion:  user%host@domain.
      With this switch enabled, the name you enter as the UUCP Domain Name
      is the domain name of your email host as illustrated in the example
      for bang domain addressing.  The generated address for this example
      using sub-domain processing will look like user.name%yourbbs@site.com.

    High Ascii
    ~~~~~~~~~~
      The Internet is made up of a conglomerate of machines.  Some of these
      machines may be PCs, Workstations, Macintosh, etc.  Not all of these
      machines are able to handle high-ASCII characters.  Therefore, PCBoard
      gives you control of how to deal with high-ASCII characters when
      exporting messages. Valid settings are:

        S       Strip all high ASCII on export, replacing with nothing
        R       Replace all high ASCII characters with a period (.)
        C       Convert all high ASCII to roughly equivalent standard ASCII
      	N	Perform no processing on high ASCII characters

UUUTIL.EXE
===============================================================================

      It's not uncommon for a system carrying Usenet conferences to handles
      hundreds or thousands of new conferences. That could entail a lot of
      extra work on behalf of the SysOp. Much of this work can be prevented
      by using a program called UUUTIL which is included with every PCBoard
      package.  Its purpose is simple; assist in the creating and setup of
      Usenet conferences.  To run UUUTIL, load the UUUTIL.EXE using any of
      the following command line parameters:

  /ACTIVE:d:\path\active
  ----------------------
      This option scans the active file (obtainable from your provider) and
      sets the appropriate newsgroup type for each newsgroup on your system.
      The active file is a UNIX formatted text file (i.e., each line ends
      with a line feed instead of a carriage return/line feed pair).

      NOTE:  This parameter does NOT create the conference it simply insures
      a conference has the proper setting (moderator versus unmoderated news
      group).  Furthermore, if moderated newsgroups are not carried, this
      switch will never have to be used because the /IMPORT switch defaults
      to an unmoderated group.

  /EXPORT:d:\path\localng.lst
  ---------------------------
      This option exports a list of all newsgroups on your system in the
      following format:

        alt.news.group1,num1
        alt.news.group2,num2

      The text to the left of the comma is the newsgroup or conference name.
      The number to the right of the comma represents the conference number.

      NOTE:  This file can be used as the input file for the /IMPORT option.

  /IMPORT:d:\path\localng.lst
  ---------------------------
      This option is used to import a list of newsgroups from a text file
      and set up the basic information for them (location of MSGS file,
      conference type, etc.)  The text file you provide must resemble the
      following format:

        alt.news.group1[,num1]
        alt.news.group2[,num2]

      In other words, the exact name of the newsgroup (like alt.bbs.pcboard)
      is the first item on the line.  Optionally, you may include a comma
      and a number if you want that newsgroup to be a specific number.  For
      example:

        alt.bbs.pcboard,100
        news.groups,200
        news.answers

      If this file was used as the import file, the following will occur:

        1. The name of conference 100 will become alt.bbs.pcboard.  The
           information for the conference is based on the conference
           specified with the /TEMPLATE parameter or conference 0 if that
           parameter is not passed.

        2. Conference 200's name is set to news.groups

        3. Because the last conference number used was 200, the news.answers
           entry will be assigned to the next number which in this case is
           201.

      Note the [,num?] portion of the example.  This allows you to specify a
      particular conference number for a particular newsgroup, or the start
      of a range for a set of newsgroups.  If /START is not used, the first
      conference attempted is 1, otherwise the specified number is used.
      Any time a conference number appears after a newsgroup name, that
      number is used as the new "start" number for that conference and all
      future conferences.  Additionally, conference 0 will never be
      overwritten and if a conference is already in use, you will be warned
      and allowed to overwrite, skip, or find the next available conference.

      To avoid situations where a single directory is completely overloaded
      with files (or other directories), this option will attempt to create
      a balanced directory tree off your base UUCP directory to hold each of
      the necessary message files for each newsgroup initialized.  It does
      this by using the first letter of each newsgroup hierarchy level and
      as much of the last level as possible.  In other words, if you have a
      base UUCP path of C:\PCB\UUCP, and you are initializing
      alt.bbs.pcboard, the MSGS file entry is specified as
      C:\PCB\UUCP\A\B\PCBOA001.

  /INDEX
  -------
      This option is used to force a rebuild of the CNAMES.IDX file.  The
      index can also be regenerated by doing a full save when exiting
      PCBSetup.

  /MODERATORS:d:\path\moderators
  ------------------------------
      By default, UUCP forwards moderated mail to a big provider like UUNET
      and lets it forward to the appropriate moderator.  Should you desire
      to control the list of moderators yourself, this parameter can be used
      to import a list of moderators into your system.  The basic format for
      this file is:

        newsgroup.name:e-mail.address

      Example:

        alt.bbs.pcboard:support@saltair.com

      The purpose of this option is to scan the moderator file given by your
      provider and add the appropriate information to the USENET.MOD file
      for each moderated newsgroup you carry. The moderators file is a UNIX
      formatted text file (i.e., each line ends with a line feed instead of
      a carriage return/ line feed pair).

  /SETNAMES:d:\path\localng.lst
  -----------------------------
      This option is of particular interest to those currently using
      third-party products to handle their UUCP needs but are looking to
      convert to PCBoard.  In this scenario it is quite likely the
      conferences are already setup and ready to go but there are a few
      items not taken care of:

        1. The conference names may not truly reflect the newsgroup they
           carry.

        2. Options in the second configuration screen determining the
           conference type among other things are not set.

      Using this option, the existing conference information is preserved
      and only necessary options are changed. This is how the option is
      useful for those seeking to convert from a previous news or e-mail
      handling program.  In all other ways, this option is identical to the
      /IMPORT option.

  /START:confnum
  --------------
      This option is used to specify the start conference number for an
      import operation.  Any conference numbers specified in the text file
      used by /IMPORT or /SETNAMES override this setting.

  /TEMPLATE:confnum
  -----------------
      Unless otherwise specified, all new conferences are based on the Main
      Board (conference 0).  To select a different conference from which to
      clone new ones from, the /TEMPLATE option must be used.  After the
      colon, simply enter the conference number to use as the cloning
      conference.

The UUCP Programs
===============================================================================

      There are three programs encompassing the normal operation of UUCP in
      PCBoard--UUOUT, UUXFER, and UUIN which export, transfer, and import
      messages respectively.  When the feed is coming from and going to a
      provider via modem, all three programs must be run from an event.  On
      the other hand, if the feed is provided from a satellite or similar
      device, only UUIN must to be run to import the messages into PCBoard.
      The following sections describe each of the main programs in the UUCP
      package.

  UUOUT.EXE
  ---------
      The process of transferring mail entered on your system back to the
      Internet involves what is called exporting mail.  This job falls to
      UUOUT which scans the PCBoard conferences for mail and prepares these
      messages in the form of packets so UUXFER can send them to the
      provider.

      Running UUOUT with no parameters is the easiest way to get things
      going as it will automatically scan all newsgroups and e-mail
      conferences.  Your situation may require special circumstances in
      which case look at the Translating Names and Selecting Conferences for
      Export topics in this section for more details.

      For most configurations, UUOUT is run by executing UUOUT.EXE with no
      parameters.  Mind you, the program must be run from a directory
      containing a valid PCBOARD.DAT file so information about the base UUCP
      path and such can be properly located.

    Translating Names
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      When exporting messages to other systems around the world, it is quite
      likely you may want to change the name used for the FROM name.  A good
      example of this is the SysOp account. As you know, PCBoard gives the
      SysOp the ability to hide behind an alias (see PCBSetup | Sysop
      Information).  In situations such as this, mail will go out being
      written by sysop@yoursite.com. What if you want to change it so you're
      real name is used? After all, how will someone half-way around the
      world know you're the SysOp of the site the message is coming from and
      will it be of concern to them?

      For purposes such as this, create an file named ALIAS.OUT and put it
      in the UUCP Base Path.  This file is a simple ASCII text file listing
      the name to look for and the replacement name to use as shown in the
      following example:

        sysop,john.doe

      This sample entry will make any message from a user titled sysop go
      out as being from john.doe.  The search for a match is not case
      sensitive.  Make note, the period in the replacement name is critical
      as the Internet does not use a space to separate names.

      The ALIAS.OUT file simply translates the major portion of the FROM
      address, it also does not update what is referred to as the short
      name, which is the name in parentheses or the name after the comma (if
      reading with PCBoard).  Let's look at a sample FROM line:

        From: sysop@yoursite.com, SYSOP

      If you use the ALIAS.OUT example, the name sysop will be translated to
      john.doe making the FROM address appear as:

        From: john.doe@yoursite.com, SYSOP

      From the viewpoint of protecting your identity this is not a good
      situation because now people can easily tell if you're the SysOp or
      not.   Luckily the -f command line parameter for UUOUT which will
      force the short name to be changed as well as the regular FROM
      address.  An example of how to execute the UUOUT program in this
      situation follows:

        UUOUT -f

    Selecting Conferences for Export
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      The default for UUOUT is to scan all conferences for mail or news to
      export.  Should your situation dictate only certain conferences be
      scanned use the -c command line parameter.  Let's start by looking at
      a few sample UUOUT command lines and discuss them so you have a better
      understanding how this works:

        UUOUT
        UUOUT -c 101
        UUOUT -c 101,103-150,160
        UUOUT -c 101-
        UUOUT -c -150

      The first line of this example set will scan all conferences because
      no -c parameter was used.  Only conference 101 will be scanned if we
      use the second line.  On the third line, conference 101, 103 through
      150, and 160 will be scanned. The last two lines illustrate how to
      setup an open ended range--line five will scan all conferences between
      101 and your highest defined conference while the last line scans
      conferences 0 to 150.

      Why would anyone want to use an open ended range? Many UUCP
      configurations will start at a particular conference number like 100
      or 101 and go to whatever number is necessary. By using an open ended
      range, you will not have to remember to update your batch files every
      time you add a new conference to the system.  This can be a very
      time-saving feature.

    File Attachments
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      If a file attachment is found, it will be automatically UUENCODED and
      transmitted as part of the message body.  Messages larger than
      approximately 64k are broken down into multi-part messages.  This is
      done because many sites limit the size of incoming e-mail messages to
      this limit.

      NOTE:  It is the responsibility of the recipient to properly DECODE
      the message to its original form.

    RFC-822 Compliancy
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

      The standard referred to as RFC822 is the prevalent format to send
      messages between Internet addresses.  When PCBoard exports the message
      this is the format it uses.  Should you want to override any of the
      typical message header information such as DISTRIBUTION, FOLLOWUP-TO,
      NEWSGROUPS, REFERENCES, REPLY-TO, SUBJECT, or TO, you may include the
      field to replace followed by a colon and the text to be used.  For
      example, to change the REPLY-TO field in a message refer to the
      following example:

        To: winbeta@microsoft.com     Subj: We're interested in testing.
       (-------------------------------------------------------------------)
        1: REPLY-TO: president@saltair.com
        2:
        3: Hello, we are interested in testing the latest beta.  Please
        4: forward appropriate information.
        5:
        6: Management

      The allowed headers are DISTRIBUTION:, FOLLOWUP-TO:, NEWSGROUPS:,
      REFERENCE:, REPLY-TO:, SUBJECT:, and TO:.  Scanning will continue for
      as long as valid headers are found.  The first invalid header line
      will be considered the first line of the message.

  UUXFER.EXE
  ----------
      The sole purpose of UUXFER is to transfer email/news to and from your
      BBS to a provider over a modem connection.  You can think of the
      program as a very specialized terminal program running a script to get
      and receive mail.  In fact, the instructions required to login to the
      provider is in what is known as the systems file.

      Before we start talking about the systems file, you must understand
      how the UUXFER program is called.  This program is used to dial a UUCP
      service provider, send outbound email and news, and receive inbound
      email and news.  In order to know which provider to call, a site must
      be specified via the -s switch.  For example:

        UUXFER -s xmission

      This example shows how UUXFER is used to call up a provider with the
      site name of xmission.  By looking at the SYSTEMS file, UUXFER will
      know how to dial the site and how to get logged in.

    Command Line Parameters
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      	-7	Start in 7 data bit, even parity, 1 stop bit
                (7E1) mode for the login connection

        -b      When calling a provider and the number is busy, UUXFER will
                normally abort back to dos.  Use of this command line
                parameter sets the number of dial attempts.

        -g      This switch gives you the ability to take advantage of file
                grading if your provider supports it.  File grading is where
                a type of communication like e-mail has a higher grade than
                that of a newsgroup article.  By specifying the grade, you
                can control what type of communications will be sent for one
                call.

        -n      Overrides the UUCP Name field in PCBSetup | UUCP
                Configuration.  This option is useful when you call multiple
                sites to exchange mail/news where your system or UUCP name
                is different for each system.  The name and -n must be
                separated by a space (e.g., -n pcboard)

      	-o 	Take the modem off hook when finished

        -s      This option controls the system name to dial. Enter a
                specific system name, all to dial every site defined in the
                SYSTEMS file, or any to dial only cites with files waiting
                to be sent.

        -t      Specifies the time-out during chat script processing (-t 60)

    Errorlevels Set Upon Exit
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

      UUXFER.EXE supports several errorlevel codes to alert you (and allow
      batch file programming) that certain events have occurred or
      conditions have been met.  Following is a list of error codes that
      will be returned:

      	0 = No errors
      	1 = Failure processing chat script
      	2 = Lost carrier after chat script completion
      	3 = Site not found in SYSTEMS file
      	4 = Invalid entry found in SYSTEMS file
      	5 = No message found during Shere exchange
      	6 = Bad message found during Shere exchange
      	7 = UUCP G protocol not available at site
      	8 = Error during UUCP G protocol

  UUIN.EXE
  --------
      This program is used to import email and news from a spool directory
      to PCBoard.  Its job is accomplished by scanning all spool directories
      for mail or news to import which is accomplished by simply typing

        UUIN

      at a DOS prompt.  You may limit the spool directory to scan by using
      the -s parameter as shown in this example:

        UUIN -s xmission

      This shows how UUIN can be used to scan just the spool directory for
      the xmission site.  Assuming the spool directory is defined as
      C:\PCB\UUCP\SPOOL, UUIN will scan C:\PCB\UUCP\SPOOL\XMISSION.

    DECOMP.BAT: How it is Used for Importing
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Many Internet providers will give the news in a format called
      compressed batch.  To import the news UUIN does require a packet to be
      in uncompressed batch form.  Although two providers may both compress
      their news packets there is no guarantee they will use the same
      programs to compress or even uncompress the packets.

      Due to a lack of a true standard, UUIN will run DECOMP.BAT (in the
      base UUCP subdirectory) whenever it examines a packet and finds out if
      it is compressed or not.  "Wait a second.  If UUIN can detect a
      compressed packet, why not just uncompress it?", you ask.  The key
      piece of information to understand is that the packet merely states
      whether it is compressed or not.  No information is provided regarding
      the type of compression used.

      With that settled, let's continue with UUIN running DECOMP.BAT.  In
      running the batch file, only the filename to uncompress is sent.  The
      assumption is made that the decompression program will automatically
      strip the extension of the filename when outputting the uncompressed
      file.

      The following shows the sample DECOMP.BAT included with the package:

        rem *** This is a sample batch file only!  Customize for your system!
        comp430d -db12 %1

    File Attachments
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      Message attachments are automatically handled by UUIN and UUOUT.  In
      other words, if a uuencoded message comes into the system, it will be
      automatically added to a message as a file attachment.  This saves a
      lot of time for the users who would otherwise have to manually encode
      the message to its original form.

      NOTE:  Many encoded messages are split into multiple parts for
      transfer.  Since there is no standard splitting the messages, there is
      no guarantee UUIN will be able to properly turn all uuencoded messages
      into file attachments.  You'll know a message was not decoded if you
      see text similar to the following example:

        M^<?[SH`^`=`%1"QSISAQFM>\[ZX!KD`,`*%`.@Q]PWC/SNP'^H4J^I`&I1*+&
        M2C9MF:^7@#]8"<F8<A/"_M>H`&8#`E%H2!-&DB;^_J98NW>X@:4NZJ=<]"/=U

    Name Translations and Aliases
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      It is not common for a business or BBS to want to create an alias to
      make it easier for others to send mail.  For example, on the PCBoard
      support system we want to allow you to always be able to send mail to
      support@saltair.com and know it will be re-addressed to someone in
      charge of support (the SysOp for example.)  To translate an incoming
      name, a file named ALIAS.IN must be created and stored in the UUCP
      base path.  The format of the file is ASCII text.  On each line is the
      incoming user name to translate followed by a comma, and finally the
      user name the message will ultimately be addressed to.  Example:

        support,bob smith
        sales,john clark

    Bouncing a Message
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      If a message is imported but an alias or user record cannot be found
      on the system for this user name, the message will be bounced.  When a
      message is bounced, notice is sent to the receiver that something was
      wrong with the message.  You may create a custom message to bounce
      back to the sender of the message by editing a file called BOUNCE.TXT
      in the base UUCP subdirectory.

      NOTE:  Additionally, messages to the following users will be imported
      regardless of their presence in the users file: postmaster, root,
      uucp, sysop, and usenet.

    Header Information in Messages
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      By default, all header lines are imported into the message body, just
      like they come in from your service provider. However, that can take
      up a lot of extra space that you might like to use for other purposes.
      For this reason, you can create a file named HDRKEYS.LST in your base
      UUCP path directory that has certain keywords to scan for to include
      in your message.  If one of the keywords matches the start of the
      header, the header will be included in the message body.  Otherwise,
      the header itself will be scanned for necessary information, but it
      won't be included in the message body within the PCBoard message base.
      So, for example, to include only the FROM:, SUBJECT:, DATE:, and
      MESSAGE-ID: headers, create a file like this:

        FROM:
        SUBJECT:
        DATE:
        MESSAGE-ID:

      UUIN can be configured to strip all headers by creating a HDRDKEYS.LST
      file which contains just the following line:

        NOHEADERS

      NOTE:  While the colons are not required, they help ensure that you
      only desired headers are kept with the messages imported into the
      system.

    Satellite Feeds
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      UUIN.EXE also supports Usenet satellite feeds such as PageSat and
      Planet Connect.  These systems transfer BAG files, which are simply
      batched (but not compressed) news files. PageSat creates *.BAG files
      directly.  Planet Connect actually creates PCUSENET.* files, which are
      zipped (compressed) *.BAG files, one per file.  To import these
      sources of news, you must unzip the PCUSENET.* files first (in the
      case of Planet Connect) and move the *.BAG files to an appropriate
      spool directory.

    Command Line Parameters
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

        -f      Force email to be imported regardless of whether or not the
                user name is found in the USERS file.

        -j      Normally when mail cannot be properly placed by UUIN (a
                conference does not exist, etc.) the mail is placed in the
                junk conference.  Use of this switch will simply ignore junk
                mail as if it had never came in.  This is useful when the
                feed comes in via satellite dish setups where it is known in
                advance that junk will be coming in as part of the feed and
                is not an error in the setup.

        -l      This switch is used to disable import of long TO names.
                While they are useful in determining exactly how the
                original message was addressed, they are not essential.
                Remember how mail will come in addressed similar to
                joe.smith@yourbbs.com, JOE SMITH?  When this switch is used,
                the message will simply be addressed to JOE SMITH.

        -o      This switch will scan the junk conference and output a list
                of newsgroups the messages originally came from. This list
                is in ASCII format and is quite usable by UUUTIL to add
                these newsgroups as new conferences.

        -s      Remember that UUIN, by default, scans all spool directories.
                To single out a specific spool directory to scan, use this
                switch followed by a space, and the name of the spool
                directory to scan.  For example, if the spool directory is
                C:\PCB\UUCP\SPOOL\XMISSION, run UUIN -s XMISSION.

        -u      Use this switch to disable UUIN's automatic decoding of file
                attachments.  Use of this switch will require the user to
                decode the message back into the original file type.
